+# Flight 77 chart units and operator interview copies
+
+November 8, 2026 Eastern · TEL01–TEL04 / AUD016-A1 / TC05–TC06. Recovered the **one-page Flight Chart for Flight 77**, Team 7 Box 13 item 212, and the **36-page FBI 302s of Interest Flight 77** packet, Team 7 Box 17, from the public Commission archive mirror. The chart documents an unresolved duration-unit discrepancy. The interview packet supplies a separate scan of an already identified FBI interview. The sources leave the operator recording, the final question transmittal and the native carrier records open. [NARA finding aid, physical p. 106](https://www.archives.gov/files/research/9-11/finding-aid.pdf#page=106) · [Box 13 file unit](https://catalog.archives.gov/id/2608332) · [Archive members and hashes](SOURCE-PINS.json)
+
+## Chart fields compared with the May 20 memo
+
+Item 212 has one physical PDF page with the printed footer **-2-**. The date and original packet assembly of this sheet remain open. These observations use physical page numbers and page-local layout-text lines.
+
+| Time or field | Box 13 chart, physical p. 1 | May 20 briefing memo, physical p. 2 |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| **9:15:34** | Lines 18–19 describe an operator-assisted call to an unknown number and give **“102 minutes (1 min. 42 secs.)”**. Both unit labels are visible on the scan. Read literally, they imply **6,120 seconds** and **102 seconds**, respectively. | Line 11 gives **102 seconds**. Keep the chart's two duration expressions and the memo's duration side by side. Their source fields and editing history remain open. |
+| **9:18:58** | Lines 11–14 place the DOJ entry under the heading naming Barbara Olson and say **“Call did not connect”**. | Line 12 gives **No Connection**. The court display separately gives **09:18:58 / 0 seconds**. No shared carrier event ID has been established. |
+| **9:25:48** | Lines 24–25 give unknown destination and **159 seconds (2 mins. 39 secs.)**. | Line 14 displays **159 seconds**. The Commission final report's **154 seconds** remains separately attributed. |
+| **9:14:57** | Lines 31–33 put an **“On” button pressed** entry under unconnected calls and say **“no call made”**. | This time label does not appear among the memo's seven displayed rows. The court presentation includes a separate button-pressed entry. |
+
+A literal label check finds **eight** distinct time labels on the chart and **seven** in the May 20 table. The chart includes all seven table labels and the additional **9:14:57** label. The memo's p. 1 prose separately refers to eight calls. The chart's 9:14:57 entry provides a concrete candidate for investigating the count difference. The observed label overlap leaves the relationship between the two versions, the intended counting rule and the actual call events unresolved. [May 20 review](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/47b5e098fb7ae161ffea0b613ae3b17fba7dad55/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-airline-reply-and-starfix-statements-2026-11-06/README.md) · [Court display verification](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/2d73a93d12276caae2b6029eff5c504bfdb8ff3b/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-zero-entry-context-verified-2026-11-02/README.md) · [Commission report notes](https://www.911commission.gov/report/911Report_Notes.htm)
+
+The right margin of item 212 contains unattributed handwritten call-attribution notes, including a tentative line about calls being dropped. Their author, date and evidentiary basis remain open. The typed note at lines 37–38 concerns recorded calls attributable to the cockpit given the stated absence of an ATC conversation with the hijackers. The May 14 request concerned a passenger-to-operator recording, a separate record class. [May 14 request, question 10](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/ba199e4b0d56c529e7d68da6646d1ab4ca36795b/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-january-presentation-and-may-doj-questions-2026-11-08/README.md)
+
+## Interview serials and copy provenance
+
+- **265A-NY-280350-302-22170 / M-INT-00000058.** Both the newly recovered Box 17 PDF and the held Box 19 Key 302s PDF put this September 11 operator-relay interview at physical p. 36, header lines 1–5. Box 17 lines 22–32 and Box 19 lines 21–31 describe a female passenger on Flight 77, a transfer request, guns and knives, and a husband described as a sergeant. They report the second operator's account of what the receiving operator heard. The page renders differ in pixels and privacy-label placement while the selected body statements and identifiers match. Count this as two archival reproductions of the same reported interview.
+- **265A-NY-280350-302-22171 / M-INT-00000059.** The held Box 19 PDF has the receiving operator's interview at p. 17. Lines 23–30 give a seatback telephone, the requested connection to a husband described as a sergeant, and guns and knives. The body does not name the passenger or flight. The handwritten identification above the form is a separate annotation of unverified authorship and date. This preserves the earlier image-based reading against a recoverable PDF.
+- **265A-NY-280350-302-4082.** The April 8 Concordance working memo, Box 21 item 267, p. 1 lines 39–43, cites this serial alongside 22171. A literal serial screen of all 36 Box 17 and all 52 held Box 19 pages located 22170 and 22171 as above and produced no 4082 hit. The missing body, its relationship to the quoted working-memo references, and any related operator tape remain unresolved. The search is limited to the available text layers. [Earlier operator source comparison](https://github.com/AveLyra/911-conspiracy-investigation/blob/06ae20cb70295e1abf22b5a0a06d4e9facf736f7/investigation/broader-evidence/telephone-operator-custodian-2026-10-27/README.md)
